[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should actually trigger a notification? → Due soon, Overdue[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Clean — both derive from [38;5;153mdueDate[39m, so no extra fields on a task and no second scheduling concept. Notifications stay a
  pure function of the task list plus a lead-time setting.

  Last question before I present a design. The repo is a static HTML file today, and "structured to grow" plus a service
  worker pushes on that.

  - [1mVanilla JS, no build step[0m — keeps the repo as-is, zero toolchain, service worker drops straight in. Costs
    hand-written DOM updates, which get unwieldy as task state grows. Adding a framework later means rewriting the view
    layer.
  - [1mVite + TypeScript, no framework[0m — a build step and types. Types are what make the data-access layer a real boundary
    rather than a convention, which is the thing you're paying for with "structured to grow." Still no framework to
    learn. Same caveat: a framework later is a view-layer rewrite.
  - [1mVite + a framework (React/Svelte)[0m — pays off when UI state gets complex, and multi-user will make it complex. Costs
    the most setup now for value you don't collect until later.

  I recommend [1mVite + TypeScript, no framework[0m. A task list's UI state is genuinely small, so a framework is the
  YAGNI-violating choice today — but the typed data layer isn't, because that boundary is exactly the thing that makes
  the eventual multi-user migration a swap instead of a rewrite. The honest caveat: this is the one choice here that
  isn't cheap to reverse, since moving to a framework later means redoing the views.
